using Godot;
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

/// res://scripts/GunCombat.cs
/// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/// PALISADE GUNPLAY — replaces SwordCombat on the player. Training bots keep
/// their swords, so SwordCombat stays live for them.
///
/// Every player spawns with a random gun from the STARTER pool and races for a
/// random gun from the ARENA pool in the middle. Both rolls are shared between
/// the two players (the host picks and broadcasts), so a match varies but is
/// never lopsided.
///
/// One verb: FIRE. Accuracy is the skill expression — spread grows with your
/// horizontal speed and while airborne, so moving fast makes you hard to hit but
/// unable to shoot straight, while standing still makes you accurate and a
/// target. The crosshair opens and closes with live spread, so the tradeoff
/// teaches itself.
///
/// ADS is scope-only: the AWP is the single gun in the pack with a scope, so it
/// is the only one that can aim down sights (tighter cone, capped move speed).
/// Every other gun ignores the aim input.
///
/// Damage is client-authoritative, matching the existing net contract: we resolve
/// hits locally, damage the opponent's puppet, and PlayerHealth.DamageDealt
/// relays {"t":"hit","dmg":X}. No new damage netcode.
///
/// Models are the Styloo pack (free, itch.io). They ship with NO animation clips,
/// but every gun is modelled in separate parts — slide/bolt, magazine, trigger,
/// and the shotgun's pump — which PlayerController.AnimateFpGun drives
/// procedurally. That stays locked to whatever fire rate and reload duration are
/// tuned here, which baked clips could not.
/// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public partial class GunCombat : Node
{
